 all at 25 G each, NO blocks at all. That means 3 alike components only get the block price when they are EXACTLY 3. If there are 4, none are blocks. So \"block of exactly 3\" means: only when the total count for that type is exactly a multiple of 3, the groups of 3 get block price.\n\nWait no — 7 = 3+3+1 or 7×25=175. 175 = 7×25, meaning no block. So it seems like: block applies for multiples of exactly 3?? No wait, 6 should be 2 blocks = 120. But the spec example says 7 = 175 so that's 7×25. \n\nLet me re-examine: maybe the block is only when the total count IS exactly 3? But then \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\" — that works: 3 runes = 60, 3 moonstones = 60, total 120.\n\nAnd yet 4 runes = 100 (4×25), 7 runes = 175 (7×25). So block applies only when the count for a type is ≤ 3? That makes no sense for other examples. \n\nHmm, let me re-read more carefully: \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" Maybe the semantics is: you can form blocks of exactly 3. For 4, you'd have one block of 3 (60) + 1 remainder (25) = 85. But spec says 100. So maybe the remaining 1 doesn't count?\n\nActually re-reading: \"no block — block requires exactly 3\". Perhaps the block is an all-or-nothing per type deal? If you have exactly 3 of one type, you get the block price. If not exactly 3, you pay per-component price? But then 7 = 175 makes sense (7×25, no block). And 4 = 100 (4×25, no block). And 3 = 60 (block).\n\nBut what about 6 runes? 6 = 2 × 3, so two blocks = 120? Or 6×25 = 150? The spec doesn't have a 6-of-a-kind example, but the logic implies: if you have exactly 3 of a type, you get the block. For any other count, per-component. That seems to be what the spec says: \"block requires exactly 3\".\n\nWait but the spec says \"Building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" and the example says \"no block — block requires exactly 3\" for 4 runes. This clearly states that blocks only apply when exactly 3 of a type.\n\nBut then 3 runes + 3 moonstones is 120 (two blocks). That reinforces exactly-3.\n\nSo the logic is: when exactly 3 of a component type, they cost 60 as a block. For any other count, each costs 25 individually. No grouping into blocks within a larger set. Block is only for exactly-3.\n\nLet me fix the implementation. For 7 runes, 7×25=175. For 4 runes, 4×25=100.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_edcb3ca5e9d44e52bae7287f","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  // Apply component block pricing: block of exactly 3 alike = 60 G\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(componentCounts)) {\n    if (count === 3) {\n      totalBase += BLOCK_PRICE;\n    } else {\n      totalBase += count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n    }\n  }","oldText":"  // Apply component block pricing\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    const blocks = Math.floor(count / 3);\n    const remainder = count % 3;\n    totalBase += blocks * BLOCK_PRICE + remainder * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-28-59_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro/src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":1065,"output":1281,"cacheRead":41216,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":43562,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784950386386,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-c627e105-b6b0-4a34-8c1f-0d724f89df2e","responseModel":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_edcb3ca5e9d44e52bae7287f","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-28-59_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro/src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n 43       totalBase += BASE_PREMIUMS[item