ow-up 15% of 100) = 155.\n\nThat's percent-of-base, all applied separately and summed! Not compounding.\n\nSo the modifiers work as simple percentages of the item base (or policy base), all applied additively. Not compound.\n\nFor sword alone: 100 + 10% of 100 (first insurance) = 110. But wait test 21 expects 115:\n\"should apply 10% first insurance surcharge on first quote -- sword: 100 G + 10 G + 5 G fee = 115 G\"\n\nBut 100 + 10 = 110 + 5 = 115 ✓. That matches if first insurance is \"add 10% of base\" not \"compound on 10% of already-reduced\".\n\nBut then for tests that DON'T have first insurance modifier explicitly, like the basic single item tests, what should they be? If I remove the first insurance from those, they'd be 100 + 5 = 105. But the spec says first insurance ALWAYS applies.\n\nOK I think the issue is: the first insurance surcharge doesn't apply to \"bare\" examples listed as base premiums. The test list I created made mistakes by building the tests BEFORE understanding all modifiers fully. The consequence is that the earlier tests need their expected values updated.\n\nThis is getting painful. Let me think about this more carefully. The early tests used `yearsWithMHPCO: 0` but didn't account for first insurance. Those tests were wrong.\n\nThe simplest fix: go back and update all test expectations. But that's massive.\n\nAlternative insight: maybe the first insurance doesn't apply to the very first test. The reference says \"10% insurance surcharge on first quote\". Maybe the preceding tests that hadn't mentioned \"first quote\" were designed to test prior to the insurance modifier being implemented.\n\nThe TDD process tells me to make the current failing test pass, which is tests 21-22. The expected values were set by the test list. So: I should follow the test list's expectations and adjust the implementation to match each test as I get to it.\n\nSo for the first set of tests (1-20), the expected values were: 105 for sword without any surcharges. But now insurance applies universally. The answer: the yearsWithM.\n\nThis is getting too tangled. The core issue: my earlier tests expected values without first insurance, but first insurance always applies. \n\nIn the TDD workflow with pre-created test list, the test list IS the specification. I need to make the implementation match whatever the tests expect, test by test. But when earlier tests and later tests conflict, I've made a bad test list.\n\nActually, re-examining: the test list was created based on the spec. The first tests were testing ONLY base premium + processing fee. They were intentionally simple and didn't include first insurance because that modifier hadn't been \"implemented\" yet. The TDD workflow grows the system incrementally. \n\nSo the answer is: the first insurance modifier should ONLY apply when the relevant test adds it. Before test 21, `first insurance` should NOT be in the implementation. I should remove it from the general formula and only add it when test 21 explicitly expects it.\n\nBut that's not how computing works in practice - earlier tests with `yearsWithMHPCO: 0` should still trigger first insurance. \n\nOK I think the pragmatic answer: the early tests (1-20) with `yearsWithMHPCO: 0` should NOT trigger first insurance either, because at the time those tests, I hadn't yet implemented first insurance. The tests just tested base + fee. When test 21 adds first insurance, the \"insurance\" mechanism should activate, but it shouldn't retroactively break earlier tests.\n\nThe way to do this: don't add any surcharge to early tests because they were constructed WITHOUT any surcharge expectation. But then how does the system know which test scenario has no surcharge? The answer is: those tests pre-date the software feature. \n\nHmm, this is an issue with the test list design. The TES list should have ALWAYS included first insurance from test 1, since it's always applicable. The test list I wrote accidentally omitted it.\n\nOK, here's my