e`: Base premium for a single item — name fits.\n- `itemTotal`: Item base plus surcharges — name fits.\n- `policyAdjustment`: Running total of policy-wide adjustments — name fits.\n- `premium`: Final computed premium — name fits.\n\nOverall naming is solid. No renames needed.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\nLet me analyze the `calculateQuotePremium` function (the main logic):\n\n```typescript\nfunction calculateQuotePremium(\n  items: { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number }[],\n  customerYears: number,\n  quoteIndex: number\n): number {\n  // Validate all item types\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n\n  let totalBase = 0;\n  let totalWithSurcharges = 0;\n  \n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      const itemBase = BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0;\n      let itemTotal = itemBase;\n      if (item.cursed) {\n        itemTotal += itemBase * 0.5;\n      }\n      if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5) {\n        itemTotal += itemBase * 0.3;\n      }\n      totalBase += itemBase;\n      totalWithSurcharges += itemTotal;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    if (count === 3) {\n      totalBase += BLOCK_PRICE;\n      totalWithSurcharges += BLOCK_PRICE;\n    } else {\n      totalBase += count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n      totalWithSurcharges += count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  let policyAdjustment = 0;\n  \n  if (customerYears >= 2) {\n    policyAdjustment -= totalBase * 0.2;\n  }\n  \n  policyAdjustment += totalBase * 0.1;\n  \n  if (quoteIndex > 0) {\n    policyAdjustment -= totalBase * 0.15;\n  }\n\n  const premium = Math.ceil(totalWithSurcharges + policyAdjustment + PROCESSING_FEE - 1e-10);\n  return premium;\n}\n```\n\n**Component Count:**\n\n**Constants:**\n- `\"Unknown item type:\"` template string (1) + template literal `${item.type}` (but that's an interpolation; counting literal strings): let me count the message as 1 constant: \"Unknown item type: \" interpolation → the literal part is 1 constant\n- `0` (initializers for totalBase, totalWithSurcharges, policyAdjustment) = 3 constants  \n- `1` (in `?? 0 + 1`) = 1\n- `0.5` (cursed surcharge) = 1\n- `5` (enchantment threshold) = 1\n- `0.3` (high enchantment surcharge) = 1\n- `3` (block count threshold) = 1\n- `COMPONENT_BASE` constant use — this is a binding reference, not literal constant\n- `BLOCK_PRICE` constant use — binding reference\n- `0` (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) = already counted\n- `2` (customerYears >= 2) = 1\n- `0.2` (loyalty discount) = 1\n- `0.1` (first insurance surcharge) = 1\n- `0` (quoteIndex > 0) = 1\n- `0.15` (follow-up discount) = 1\n- `1e-10` = 1\n- `PROCESSING_FEE` = binding reference (constant declared above)\n\nTotal constants: Let me be more precise. Literal values:\n- 0 (3 times) = 3\n- 1 (once) = 1\n- 0.5 = 1\n- 5 = 1  \n- 0.3 = 1\n- 3 = 1\n- 2 = 1\n- 0.2 = 1\n- 0.1 = 1\n- 0 (in quoteIndex > 0) = 1\n- 0.15 = 1\n- 1e-10 = 1\n- String literal part = 1\n\nTotal constants: 15\n\n**Bindings:**\n- `items`, `customerYears`, `quoteIndex` (parameters) = 3\n- `item` (loop variable, 2 different for loops) = 2\n- `totalBase` = 1\n- `totalWithSurcharges` = 1\n- `componentCounts` = 1\n- `count` (loop variable) = 1\n- `itemBase` = 1\n- `itemTotal` = 1\n- `policyAdjustment` = 1\n- `premium` = 1\n\nTotal bindings: 13\n\n**Invocations:**\n- `ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type)` = 2 (has + property access on ALL_ITEM_TYPES)\n- `` `Unknown item type: ${item.type}` `` = 2 (template string + item.type access)\n- `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)` = 2\n- `Object.values(componentCounts)` = 2\n- `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type]` = 2 (index access)\n- `??` nullish coalescing = 2\n- `Math.ceil(...)` = 2\n- `+` (addition in final line) = several: `totalWithSurcharges + policyAdjustment + PROCESSING_FEE - 1e-10` = 3 additions/subtractions → 6\n- `itemBase * 0.5` = 2 (multiplication)\n- `itemBase * 0.3` = 2\n- `count * COMPONENT_BASE` = 2\n- `totalBase * 0.2` = 2\n- `totalBase * 0.1` = 2\n- `totalBase * 0.15` = 2\n- `totalBase += ...` etc are assignments, not invocations\n\nLet me recount more carefully. Each operator call counts as invocation:\n\nFor loop 1 (validation):\n- `ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type)` → `.has` is invocation (2), `item.type` is property access (adders to binding `item`)\n\nHmm, let me simplify. Per the APP specification:\n- **Invocation (Mass: 2)**: Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`)\n\nSo operator calls like `+`, `*` — actually, these are operators, not function calls, in JavaScript. But in the APP spec, both `Math.max()` and `reduce()` count as invocations. Since operators like `+`, `*`, `>=`, `??` are not function calls per se but are actual \"invocations\" of operators, I'll treat them as invocations.\n\nLet me recount:\n- `ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type)` — 1 invocation (`.has`) = 2\n- `${item.type}` template literal — 1 invocation (string interpolation) = 2\n- `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)` — 1 = 2\n- `(componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1` — `??` = 1, `+` = 1 → 4\n- `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0` — `??` = 1 → 2\n- `itemBase * 0.5` — `*` = 1 → 2\n- `itemBase * 0.3` — `*` = 1 → 2\n- `totalBase += itemBase` — `+=` = assignment not invocation\n- `totalWithSurcharges += itemTotal` — assignment\n- `Object.values(componentCounts)` — 1 = 2\n- `count === 3` — `===` invocation? Hmm, comparison operators... I'll count `===` as conditional, not invocation\n- `count * COMPONENT_BASE` — `*` = 1 → 2\n- `customerYears >= 2` — comparison (conditional)\n- `totalBase * 0.2` — `*` = 1 → 2\n- `totalBase * 0.1` — `*` = 1 → 2\n- `quoteIndex > 0` — comparison (conditional)\n- `totalBase * 0.15` — `*` = 1 → 2\n- `totalWithSurcharges + policyAdjustment + PROCESSING_FEE - 1e-10` — `+`, `+`, `-` = 3 → 6\n- `Math.ceil(...)` — 1 = 2\n\nThat's complex. Let me count from the actual lines:\n\nLine: `ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type)` — has() invocation, item.type property access = 2\nLine: `` `Unknown item type: ${item.type}` `` — template literal = 2  \nLine: `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)` — has() invocation = 2\nLine: `(componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1` — `??`, `+` = 2+2 = 4\nLine: `BASE_PREMIUMS[item.type] ?? 0` — `??` = 2\nLine: `itemBase * 0.5` — `*` = 2\nLine: `itemTotal += itemBase * 0.5` — `+` (through +=) and `*`... actually += is assignment, only `*` counts = 2\nLine: `item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5` — comparisons, not invocations\nLine: `itemBase * 0.3` — `*` = 2\nLine: `Object.values(componentCounts)` — 2\nLine: `count * COMPONENT_BASE` — `*` = 2\nLine: `totalBase * 0.2` — `*` = 2\nLine: `totalBase * 0.1` — `*` = 2\nLine: `totalBase * 0.15` — `*` = 2\nLine: `totalWithSurcharges + policyAdjustment + PROCESSING_FEE - 1e-10` — +, +, - = 6\nLine: `Math.ceil(...)` — 2\n\nTotal invocations: 2+2+2+4+2+2+2+2+2+2+2+2+2+6+2 = 38\n\n**Conditionals:**\n- `if (!ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type))` — 4\n- `if (COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type))` — 4\n- `if (item.cursed)` — 4\n- `if (item.enchantment !== undefined && item.enchantment >= 5)` — `&&` combines two but it's one conditional block. Let me count the `&&` as additional conditional? Actually APP says conditionals are if/switch/?: — each if block counts. `&&` is a logical operator, could be counted as invocation or conditional... Let me treat it as conditional since it's control flow.\n  - Actually, each `if` statement counts as 1 conditional = 4. The `!ALL_ITEM_TYPES.has(...)` within the if is the condition expression. The `&&` connects two conditions inside a single `if`. I'll count the `&&` as 1 conditional (like a combined condition) — but traditionally in APP it's 1 conditional per `if` block. Let me keep it simple: each `if` = 4.\n- `if (count === 3)` — 4\n- `if (customerYears >= 2)` — 4\n- `if (quoteIndex > 0)` — 4\n\nTotal conditionals: 6 × 4 = 24\n\n**Loops:**\n- `for (const item of items)` — 2 loops (validation + main processing) = 10\n- `for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts))` — 5\n\nTotal loops: 3 × 5 = 15\n\n**Assignments:**\n- `totalBase = 0` — initialization counts? Assignment is mutation. Initial assignment... I think it counts.\n- `totalWithSurcharges = 0` — 6\n- `componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1` — 6\n- `itemTotal = itemBase` — 6\n- `itemTotal += itemBase * 0.5` — 6\n- `itemTotal += itemBase * 0.3` — 6\n- `totalBase += itemBase` — 6\n- `totalWithSurcharges += itemTotal` — 6\n- `totalBase += BLOCK_PRICE` — 6\n- `totalWithSurcharges += BLOCK_PRICE` — 6\n- `totalBase += count * COMPONENT_BASE` — 6\n- `totalWithSurcharges += count * COMPONENT_BASE` — 6\n- `policyAdjustment = 0` — 6\n- `policyAdjustment -= totalBase * 0.2` — 6\n- `policyAdjustment += totalBase * 0.1` — 6\n- `policyAdjustment -= totalBase * 0.15` — 6\n- `premium = Math.ceil(...)` — 6\n\nTotal assignments: 17 × 6 = 102\n\nPlus module-level:\n- `PROCESSING_FEE = 5` — assignment 6\n- `BASE_PREMIUMS` — object literal = assignment 6\n- `COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set(...)` — assignment 6\n- `ALL_ITEM_TYPES = new Set(...)` — assignment 6\n- `COMPONENT_BASE = 25` — assignment 6\n- `BLOCK_PRICE = 60` — assignment 6\n\nModule assignments: 6 × 6 = 36\n\nAlso module constants:\n- Literals in module scope: `\"rune\"`, `\"moonstone\"`, `\"sword\"`, `\"amulet\"`, `\"staff\"`, `\"potion\"` (6 in Set), also `100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25` (in object) = 12, `5`, `25`, `60` (not the bindings, the right-hand sides):\n- \"rune\" (2x), \"moonstone\" (2x), \"sword\", \"amulet\", \"staff\", \"potion\" = 8 string constants\n- 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25 (6 numeric constants), plus 5, 25, 60 = 9 numeric constants\n- Total module constants: 8+9 = 17\n\nModule bindings (const declarations): PROCESSING_FEE (1), BASE_PREMIUMS (1), COMPONENT_TYPES (1), ALL_ITEM_TYPES (1), COMPONENT_BASE (1), BLOCK_PRICE (1) = 6\n\nHmm this is getting very tedious in my head. Let me just formalize the overall approach:\n\n**Total Mass ≈**: \n- Module level: 6 bindings (6) + 17 constants (17) + 6 init assignments (36) + `Set()` invocations (4) + `Object.values` invocation (0 at module level) + `new Set`... actually each `new Set(...)` is an invocation = 2 each, and the constructor call. So 2 `new Set` = 4\n- Module level subtotal: 6 + 17 + 36 + 4 = 63\n\n- Function `calculateQuotePremium`:\n  - Constants: ~15 × 1 = 15\n  - Bindings: ~13 × 1 = 13\n  - Invocations: ~19 operator/function calls × 2 = 38\n  - Conditionals: ~6 × 4 = 24\n  - Loops: ~3 × 5 = 15\n  - Assignments: ~17 × 6 = 102\n  - Subtotal: 15 + 13 + 38 + 24 + 15 + 102 = 207\n\n- Function `processScenario`:\n  - Bindings: `results` (1), `quoteIndex` (1), `step` (1) = 3\n  - Constants: `0` (initializer for quoteIndex) = 1\n  - Invocations: `step.op === \"quote\"` (= already counted as conditional?), `calculateQuotePremium(...)` = 2, `results.push(...)` = 2 = 4\n  - Conditionals: `if (step.op === \"quote\")` = 4\n  - Loops: `for (const step of input.steps)` = 5\n  - Assignments: `results.push` is assignment... hmm it mutates the array. `quoteIndex++` = 6, `results = []` = 6, let's say 2 assignments = 12\n  - Subtotal: 3 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 5 + 12 = 29\n\n- `ScenarioResult` type, `ScenarioInput` type, interfaces: these are type-level, no mass.\n\n**Grand Total Mass: ~63 + 207 + 29 = ~299**\n\nNow let me think about what improvements I can make.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 1: Tests Pass ✓ (verified)\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nThe code is reasonably well-named. One thing I notice:\n\nThe `calculateQuotePremium` function does two related but distinct things:\n1. Validates item types\n2. Calculates the premium\n\nAlso, there's some complexity in the component counting and non-component processing that could be restructured.\n\nThe policy adjustments at the end are applied to `totalBase` (not totalWithSurcharges) which is a subtlety that's only visible through a comment. This could be made clearer.\n\nThe overall flow is right but could be structured more cleanly.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n\nI notice duplication patterns:\n- `totalBase += ...` and `totalWithSurcharges += ...` appear in pairs in the component processing loop — they always track together.\n- The component processing has `totalBase += ...` / `totalWithSurcharges += ...` repeated in both branches of the if/else.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\nThe init of `policyAdjustment = 0` then immediately doing `policyAdjustment -= ...` and `policyAdjustment += ...` — could this be simplified? Not really, the 10% surcharge is unconditional.\n\nOne thing I can definitely improve: combine the two parallel sums (`totalBase` and `totalWithSurcharges`) in the component loop where they mirror each other exactly. The DRY violation here is clear.\n\nLet me implement the refactoring:\n\n**Refactoring plan**: Extract the component `totalBase += value; totalWithSurcharges += value` pattern where both track the same amount.\n\nLooking at the component processing:\n```typescript\n    if (count === 3) {\n      totalBase += BLOCK_PRICE;\n      totalWithSurcharges += BLOCK_PRICE;\n    } else {\n      totalBase += count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n      totalWithSurcharges += count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n    }\n```\n\nThese always add the same value to both. I can compute once and add to both:\n\n```typescript\n    const componentAmount = count === 3 ? BLOCK_PRICE : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n    totalBase += componentAmount;\n    totalWithSurcharges += componentAmount;\n```\n\nThis eliminates duplication and clarifies that components have the same base and total value (no per-component surcharges).\n\nLet me also look at whether the `PROCESSING_FEE` is unused... no, it's used.\n\nI could also consider extracting the item validation into a separate named functi